🔴🟡🟢 Week 2 — Arrays
Deeper into C: how data is stored in memory, how strings work under the hood, and how programs receive input from the command line.

Key Concepts
•	Compiling pipeline: preprocessing → compiling → assembling → linking
•	Debugging with debug50 and printf statements
•	Arrays: fixed-size collections of same-type values
•	Array indexing starts at 0
•	Strings are arrays of characters (char[]) ending with '\0' (null terminator)
•	String functions: strlen(), toupper(), tolower(), isalpha(), isdigit()
•	Command-line arguments: argc (count), argv (values array)
•	Exit codes — 0 means success, non-zero means error
•	Cryptography basics — how simple ciphers work

Libraries Used
•	<string.h> — string manipulation
•	<ctype.h> — character classification
•	<cs50.h> — CS50 input helpers

Problem Set 2
•	scrabble — Compare scrabble word scores
•	readability — Coleman-Liau readability index
•	caesar or substitution — Cipher encryption

 
🔴🟡🟢 Week 3 — Algorithms
How do you search for something? How do you sort a list? How do you measure how fast (or slow) your solution is? This week answers all three.

Searching
•	Linear Search — check each element one by one. O(n) worst case
•	Binary Search — only works on sorted data; repeatedly halves the search space. O(log n)

Sorting
•	Bubble Sort — repeatedly swap adjacent elements if out of order. O(n²)
•	Selection Sort — find the smallest element, place it first, repeat. O(n²)
•	Merge Sort — recursively split, sort, then merge. O(n log n). Much faster for large datasets

Big O Notation
Notation	Meaning
O(1)	Constant time — same speed regardless of input size
O(log n)	Logarithmic — binary search
O(n)	Linear — linear search
O(n log n)	Log-linear — merge sort
O(n²)	Quadratic — bubble sort, selection sort

Other Concepts
•	Recursion — a function that calls itself (with a base case to stop)
•	struct / typedef struct — group related data into a custom type
•	Omega (Ω) = best case; Theta (Θ) = tight bound; O = worst case

Problem Set 3
•	sort — Identify which algorithm is which from timing data
•	plurality — Plurality voting system
•	runoff or tideman — Ranked-choice / Condorcet voting

 
🔴🟡🟢 Week 4 — Memory
The most conceptually challenging week. You learn what memory actually is, how pointers work, and how to manage memory manually.

Key Concepts
•	Hexadecimal (0x...) — base-16 numbering used to represent memory addresses
•	Memory address — every variable lives at a specific address in RAM
•	Pointer (*) — a variable that stores a memory address
•	Address-of operator (&) — gives you the address of a variable
•	Pointer arithmetic — move through memory address by address
•	Strings are char* — a pointer to the first character

Dynamic Memory Allocation
•	malloc() — allocate a block of memory on the heap
•	calloc() — like malloc but zeroes the memory
•	realloc() — resize an existing allocation
•	free() — release memory when done (forgetting = memory leak)

Memory Layout
•	Stack — local variables, function calls (managed automatically)
•	Heap — dynamically allocated memory (managed by you)
•	Buffer overflow — writing past the end of an array (security risk)
•	Valgrind — tool to detect memory leaks

File I/O
•	fopen() / fclose() — open and close files
•	fread() / fwrite() — read and write binary data
•	JPEG/BMP file structures — files have headers describing their format

Problem Set 4
•	volume — Modify audio file volume by editing raw bytes
•	filter — Image filters: grayscale, sepia, blur, edge-detect
•	recover — Recover JPEG files from a raw memory card image

 
🔴🟡🟢 Week 5 — Data Structures
Now that you understand pointers and memory, you can build your own data structures from scratch in C.

Linked Lists
•	A chain of nodes, each containing data and a pointer to the next node
•	Dynamic size — can grow and shrink unlike arrays
•	Singly linked: each node points forward only
•	Doubly linked: each node points forward and backward
•	Insert: O(1) at head. Search: O(n). Delete: O(n)

Hash Tables
•	Array of buckets; a hash function maps a key to a bucket index
•	Best case: O(1) insert, search, delete
•	Collision handling — chaining (linked list in each bucket) or probing

Tries
•	Tree where each node represents a character (prefix tree)
•	Used for autocomplete, dictionaries
•	All operations O(k) where k = key length

Trees
•	Binary Search Tree (BST) — left child < parent < right child
•	Balanced BST: O(log n) insert, search, delete

Queues and Stacks
•	Queue — FIFO (first in, first out). Like a line at a store
•	Stack — LIFO (last in, first out). Like a pile of plates

Complexity Summary
Structure	Search
Array	O(n)
Linked List	O(n)
Hash Table	O(1) avg
Trie	O(k)
BST (balanced)	O(log n)

Problem Set 5
•	inheritance — Simulate genetic trait inheritance
•	speller — Spell checker using a custom hash table

 
🔴🟡🟢 Week 6 — Python
Python does in one line what C does in ten. You already know programming — now you learn a cleaner, higher-level language.

Python vs C — Key Differences
•	No semicolons, no curly braces — indentation defines code blocks
•	Dynamic typing — no need to declare variable types
•	No manual memory management — Python handles it automatically
•	Strings are objects with methods: .upper(), .lower(), .strip(), .split()

Key Concepts
•	if / elif / else — same logic as C, different syntax
•	for loops: for item in list — iterate directly over collections
•	Functions: def name(params): return value
•	Lists [] — like arrays, but dynamic and mixed types allowed
•	Tuples () — immutable lists
•	Dicts {} — key-value pairs (like hash tables)
•	Sets {} — unique values only
•	import and modules — reuse code from other files and libraries
•	File I/O with open() — read and write files in plain Python
•	Regular expressions (re module) — pattern matching in strings
•	Exception handling: try / except — catch and handle errors gracefully
•	OOP basics: classes, objects, methods, __init__
•	sys.argv — command-line arguments in Python

Problem Set 6 (Python ports)
•	hello, mario, cash/credit, readability — re-implement C psets in Python
•	dna — DNA profile matching using Short Tandem Repeats (STRs)

 
🔴🟡🟢Week 7 — SQL
Data lives in databases. SQL lets you store, query, and manipulate structured data with a declarative syntax — you describe what you want, not how to get it.

Core SQL Commands
•	SELECT — retrieve data from a table
•	INSERT INTO — add a new row
•	UPDATE — modify existing rows
•	DELETE FROM — remove rows

Filtering and Sorting
•	WHERE — filter rows by condition
•	LIKE — pattern match (% is wildcard)
•	IN — match against a list of values
•	BETWEEN — range condition
•	ORDER BY — sort results
•	GROUP BY — group rows for aggregation
•	HAVING — filter groups (like WHERE but for GROUP BY)

Aggregations
•	COUNT() — count rows
•	SUM() — total of a column
•	AVG() — average
•	MIN() / MAX() — smallest / largest

Joins
•	JOIN / INNER JOIN — rows that match in both tables
•	LEFT JOIN — all rows from left table, matching rows from right
•	Subqueries — a SELECT inside another SELECT

Performance and Security
•	Indexes — speed up searches on a column (like a book index)
•	SQL Injection — attacker injects SQL via user input. Always use parameterized queries
•	Python + SQL with cs50: db.execute('SELECT * FROM t WHERE id = ?', id)

Problem Set 7
•	songs — Analyze Spotify song data
•	movies — Query an IMDb movie database
•	fiftyville — Solve a mystery purely with SQL queries

 
Week AI — Artificial Intelligence
A conceptual overview of AI: how machines learn, how language models work, and how to use them effectively.

Key Concepts
•	Generative AI — AI that produces new content (text, images, code)
•	Large Language Models (LLMs) — trained on massive text data; predict the next token
•	Prompt Engineering — crafting inputs to get useful outputs from AI
•	System prompts vs. user prompts — system sets context; user asks the question
•	Decision Trees — tree-shaped structure of if/else decisions
•	Minimax algorithm — game AI that minimizes the opponent's best move
•	Machine Learning — systems that learn from data rather than explicit rules
•	Reinforcement Learning — agent learns by trial and reward
•	Deep Learning — neural networks with many layers
•	Transformer Architecture — the architecture behind modern LLMs (attention mechanism)

No problem set this week — it is a supplemental/conceptual week.

 
🔴🟡🟢 Week 8 — HTML, CSS, JavaScript
The front end of the web. You learn how browsers receive, render, and interact with web pages.

How the Internet Works
•	IP address — unique identifier for every device on a network
•	DNS — translates domain names (google.com) to IP addresses
•	TCP/IP — protocols that guarantee data delivery
•	HTTP/HTTPS — application layer protocols for transferring web pages

HTML
•	HyperText Markup Language — defines the structure and content of a page
•	Tags: <html>, <head>, <body>, <div>, <p>, <h1>-<h6>, <a>, <img>, <form>, <input>
•	Attributes give tags extra info: href, src, id, class, type, name
•	Forms collect user input and send it to a server

CSS
•	Cascading Style Sheets — controls visual appearance
•	Selectors: element (p), class (.name), id (#name)
•	Box model: content → padding → border → margin
•	Flexbox — modern layout system for aligning and distributing elements
•	Responsive design — pages that look good on all screen sizes

JavaScript
•	Runs in the browser; makes pages interactive
•	DOM manipulation: document.querySelector(), getElementById()
•	Events: addEventListener('click', function) — respond to user actions
•	fetch() API — make HTTP requests from the browser without reloading the page
•	JSON — lightweight data format for sending/receiving data: JSON.parse(), JSON.stringify()
•	Async JS — code that waits for a response without freezing the page

Problem Set 8
•	trivia — Trivia quiz webpage with HTML, CSS, JS
•	homepage — Personal homepage using all three technologies

 
🔴🟡🟢 Week 9 — Flask
Build real, dynamic web applications with Python. Flask connects a Python backend to HTML frontend templates.

Key Concepts
•	Flask — a minimal Python web framework
•	Routes — map URL paths to Python functions using @app.route('/path')
•	HTTP GET — retrieve a page. HTTP POST — submit data to the server
•	Jinja2 templates — HTML files with Python logic embedded: {{ variable }}, {% for %}, {% if %}
•	Template inheritance — a base layout that other pages extend
•	request.form — access data submitted via a POST form
•	Sessions and cookies — store user-specific data across requests
•	redirect() — send user to a different URL
•	render_template() — render an HTML template and return it
•	SQLite with Flask — use cs50's SQL() to query a database from Python
•	REST APIs — design pattern for web services using HTTP methods

Basic Flask Pattern
from flask import Flask, render_template, request, redirect, session from cs50 import SQL  app = Flask(__name__) db = SQL("sqlite:///database.db")  @app.route("/", methods=["GET", "POST"]) def index():     if request.method == "POST":         # handle form submission         pass     return render_template("index.html")

